A||||13||ggarsk||Drive axle replacement||||1||
Z||000000||ggarsk||01-21-2000||11:02 PM||||I'm broke the drivers side axle on my '84 SE. I'm going to replace it tomorrow,so I was wondering if there are any tips or tricks to know to make the job easier. I tried 2 shops to see if I could get the job done. I think everybody knows the answers I got. NO...to hard to work on,can't find parts, I don't work on Fiero's. Thanks in advance,Glenn||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||01-22-2000||10:27 AM||||There was a thread about this recently, so you might want to use the search function.||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||01-22-2000||10:43 AM||||I haven't done it myself, but I've read that <BR>if you don't drain the transaxle first, you'll get oil on the floor when you pull the<BR>axle out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||01-22-2000||12:24 PM||||Just put an oil drain pan under the axle seal area to catch any oil drainage, then top the tranny off when you're done. Quicker and easier. I recommend replacing the seal too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-22-2000||02:10 PM||||Not very hard at all..... It's late but maybe this will help if you get stuck anywhere.<P>take off the wheel. remove the center cap and put it back on or mount the spare. put the car back on the ground. ((I know it's extra work sounding but it makes the next stap easier by allot.)) Set the e-brake as well.<P>put a 30mm socket on the axle nut and remove the nut. Having the wheel on and e brake set keeps everything from bouncing. you will likely need a big breaker bar or 3-4 feet of steel pipe to get this nut loose.<P>The nut is a lock nut. It will stay hard to turn all the way off and on. ((You should replace this nut. not reuse it.)) WARNING the nut will get HOT.<P>likely you will need to carefully remove the splash ring from the old axle to reuse on the new one. be carefull not to bend it up. an old wood chisle is the best tool I've found to separate the thing without trashing it.<P>When you replace the seal be careful not to markup the seal seat in the tranny. if you scratch the seat the seal will leak. You can use a VERY light coat of RTV or better yet a paint on gasket sealer to fill a small scratch.<P>While its appart carefully inspect the hub unit. replace the hub if it's lost grease out the seals. Note! the hub unit is suposed to have a certain amount of play in it. Don't assume it bad because of a tiny amount of play. If it's lost grease, or feels likel it rumbling or catching as it turn definatly replace it. If you aren't sure and cant find someone who is, then replace it for safety. It's not worth risking a failure.<P>When you final tighten the axle nut do it with the wheel on. you need quite allot of load on this nut.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ggarsk||01-22-2000||08:21 PM||||Thanks to all that answered. I did a seach before I opened this topic....nothing came up to answer what I was looking for. Ogre your post wasn't to late. I found out that I didn't have a big enough socket for the hub nut and didn't know what size it was. That saves me from buying several sizes hoping to get the right one. So it's back to work on it tomorrow,but I was able to do other maintance  on it so the day wasn't wasted. Thanks again all and have a good evening ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||01-23-2000||01:57 PM||||You can get a loaner socket from AutoZone.  You pay for it when you get it and get your money back when you return it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ggarsk||01-29-2000||05:09 PM||||After a week of not being about to work on my car I'm back at it. Now the question is ...how do you get the axle out of the tranny? According to the manuals you are just supposed to be able to pry it out...yea sure! Prying on it doesn't seem to budge it. I need to get it running...I'm tired of getting rides to work with others.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||01-29-2000||08:34 PM||||you may need a solid bar, and it needs to be against something solid. DO NOT pry on the Al tranny parts. It can be very annoying when it wants to, but there isn't much holding it. There is just a spring ring on the CV shaft. Works like a snap on a coat only stronger. The trick is to pull/pry straight. If you are loading sideways it makes it real hard.<P>if the only thing you can line up on is the tranny then use a block to spread the load. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||ggarsk||01-29-2000||11:25 PM||||Thanks Ogre...I'll try that in the morning. Have a good evening.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DRH||01-30-2000||12:30 AM||||I've had some luck tapping with a hammer also. Like ogre said be careful of the aluminum tranny case though.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||ggarsk||02-12-2000||04:27 PM||||Here's some nut sizes involved when changing your drive axles.... 3o mm for the hub nut,15/16 for the struts,50 Torx for the brake calipers,18mm for the e-brake arm,15mm for the lower ball joint bolt and 19 mm for the tie rod end. I found out that a 1 13/16 1 inch drive socket works great for driving the seals in. On the pass side if you put it on a long pipe and have somebody hold the seal and socket up while you drive it in....you can get a better swing for outside the car. I hope this helps anyone that needs to replace thier axles.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||02-13-2000||11:40 AM||||All you should have to remove is the pinch bolt from the ball joint and the brake caliper. set the rotor out of the way so it doesn't fall. After that there is enough play in the tierod and strut to allow removal of the shaft. This is all I've ever had to do on Fiero or FWD's. If you fool with the strut you have to get the car alligned.<P>Buy a seal/bearing driver. they aren't very expencive any you'll need it again for front wheel bearings, tranny seals, etc. I did without one of these for years untill I finally got tired of fighting with other options. I wish I had just bought the damn tool a long time ago. When you use it to drive a race remember to grease the drive cone on the tool with fresh greese for each race. This prevents dirt damaging the race.||||reg||1
Z||000013||dogtired||02-15-2000||03:39 AM||||Pulled short axle (left side) form a 4cyl 5speed and put it in a V6 5speed. After unhooking everything I used a 4ft long crow bar from the top. One quick snap movement between the axle and tranny case,  they both came out easily??<BR>At the time I didn't think about tranny case damage?? I may have been lucky? But didn't damage anything. And all works well???<BR>Good Luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
